Q2 - decisive arms, before the law (private temp world + one repo arm removed)
  temp destination outside the repo : rc=0 2 bytes class=tee in-census=False
  repo destination (tmp/ inside repo): rc=0 2 bytes class=tee in-census=False
                                      under-qa=False, verify_written clean
  pre-commit gate filter            : qa-prefix only (evidence face reads
                                      staged qa/*.log)
  residue after the arm             : none (directory removed, proved absent)
  so a .log at a repo path outside qa/ landed in the healthy dialect and no
  reader enumerated it - the same class of defect as R3213 and R3214, one
  layer lower.

Q3 - three closeout options and their measured cost
  (a) refuse every non-QA directory   : would red 5 shipped call sites
      (battery_shard QA_DIR, 4 qa_log selftest temp writes) and every suite
      world - the tests cannot be starved of a private world
  (b) refuse only "realpath inside repo and != QA": cost 0, because the one
      production site passes QA_DIR itself and every suite world is
      tempfile.mkdtemp (outside the repo)
  (c) record only                     : cost 0, no guard
  chosen: (b), plus the default resolved at call time (directory=None), so a
  suite that points QA at a private world cannot end up aiming at the live qa/.

LANDED (tools/qa_log.py)
  check_destination(directory): realpath + normcase of the destination and of
  QA and REPO, read at call time; QA passes, a repo path that is not QA raises
  E_DEST_INSIDE_REPO naming the three readers; a path outside the repo passes.
  write_atomic calls it first, so both write faces inherit one choke point.
  do_write(name, source_bytes, directory=None) resolves QA at call time.
  No other face moved: check_name, check_log_suffix, read_source, normalize,
  mark_carried_proofs, count_carried_proofs, count_bare_proofs, classify,
  verify_written, do_check and the CLI are byte-identical in behaviour.

